{
  "analysis": "We have a fresh shell at /app. The task is to cache the `albert/albert-base-v2` model and tokenizer from HuggingFace for offline use. The earlier attempt failed because of disk space issues on the cache mount point (tmpfs/overlayfs). I need to first inspect the environment: check available tooling (python, huggingface_hub/transformers), check disk space on the cache location, then download.",
  "plan": "1. Check disk space with df -h to see which partitions are available and their sizes. 2. Identify the default HuggingFace cache location (~/.cache/huggingface) and confirm it sits on a partition with enough space for the model (~3x expected size ~ 100-300MB, so a few GB is plenty). 3. Verify python3 and huggingface library availability. 4. Then proceed to cache the model and tokenizer.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo HOME=$HOME; ls -la ~/.cache 2>/dev/null | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 --version; python3 -c \"import huggingface_hub; print('hub', huggingface_hub.__version__)\" 2>&1 | head\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding from df -h: the default HuggingFace cache lives at /root/.cache, which is a tmpfs of only 100M total, with 75M ALREADY USED (by the earlier failed partial download) and just 25M free. This is exactly the trap the task warns about — a tmpfs default cache that can't hold the model. The root filesystem and /logs are 100% full (0 avail), so I cannot use a real disk either. I'm root, so I can either clean the partial download or enlarge the tmpfs mount. I need to gather a bit more info (partial download size, available RAM, transformers availability, network access) before downloading.",
  "plan": "1. Inspect the partial download in /root/.cache/huggingface and check available memory (tmpfs is RAM-backed, so I need RAM headroom). 2. Confirm transformers is available and that I can reach HuggingFace. 3. Enlarge the /root/.cache tmpfs to a safe size (e.g., 2G) so it can comfortably hold 2-3x the model size, satisfying the 'verify target partition can hold 2-3x' requirement. 4. Then download the model and tokenizer. Starting with inspection.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /root/.cache/huggingface 2>/dev/null; ls -la /root/.cache/huggingface/\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "free -h\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"import transformers; print('transformers', transformers.__version__)\" 2>&1 | head -1\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -sI --max-time 10 https://huggingface.co/albert/albert-base-v2/resolve/main/config.json | head -3\n",
      "duration": 11.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Findings: /root/.cache is a 100M tmpfs with 75M already consumed by the earlier partial download (the exact trap). `free` and `curl` aren't available, but python3 with huggingface_hub 1.21.0 and transformers 5.12.1 is. The root filesystem and /logs are 100% full (0 avail), so they're useless; the tmpfs is RAM-backed, so I have headroom if RAM allows. I need to check RAM via /proc/meminfo, clean the partial download, enlarge the cache tmpfs to safely hold 2-3x the model (albert-base-v2 is ~90MB, so 3x is ~270MB).",
  "plan": "1. Read /proc/meminfo for available RAM. 2. Remove the 75M partial download to free space in the tmpfs. 3. Remount /root/.cache tmpfs with size=2G to give safe headroom. 4. Confirm the new df -h shows the larger size. Then I'll run the download.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -E 'MemTotal|MemAvailable|MemFree' /proc/meminfo\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -rf /root/.cache/huggingface/hub\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount -o remount,size=2G /root/.cache 2>&1; echo exit=$?\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /root/.cache\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Good news: there's plenty of RAM (44GB available), so a RAM-backed tmpfs is viable. The remount of /root/.cache failed with 'permission denied' — this container restricts remount. But /root/.cache itself is now empty (100M free tmpfs) after cleaning the partial download. The root fs and /logs are 100% full, so no real disk. I'll try to mount a fresh, larger tmpfs at a subdirectory I create, to safely hold 2-3x the model (~270MB+ headroom).",
  "plan": "1. Create a mountpoint /root/.cache/hfcache inside the existing tmpfs. 2. Try mounting a fresh tmpfs of size=2G there. 3. If that works, set HF_HOME to it and verify df. If mount is also denied, fall back to using the existing 100M tmpfs (the model is ~90MB, fits within 100M, but I'll proceed carefully). Let me test the mount.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /root/.cache/hfcache && mount -t tmpfs -o size=2G tmpfs /root/.cache/hfcache 2>&1; echo exit=$?\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /root/.cache/hfcache\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

